👉 A data type specifies the type of data a variable can store and the amount of memory allocated for that data.
________________________________
| Data Type | Memory (Typical) |
| --------- | ---------------: |
| `char`    |           1 Byte |
| `int`     |          4 Bytes |
| `float`   |          4 Bytes |
| `double`  |          8 Bytes |
--------------------------------

Primary Data Types
------------------
1. int => The int data type stores whole numbers (integers).
          int age = 25;

2. float => The float data type stores decimal (floating-point) numbers.
            float price = 99.99;

3. double => The double data type stores double-precision floating-point numbers.
             double pi = 3.1415926535;

4. char => The char data type stores a single character.
           char grade = 'A';
________________________________
| Data Type | Format Specifier |
| --------- | ---------------- |
| `int`     | `%d`             |
| `char`    | `%c`             |
| `float`   | `%f`             |
| `double`  | `%lf`            |
--------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    int main()
    {
        int age = 21;
        float salary = 35000.50;
        double pi = 3.1415926535;
        char grade = 'A';
        printf("Age = %d\n", age);
        printf("Salary = %f\n", salary);
        printf("PI = %lf\n", pi);
        printf("Grade = %c\n", grade);
        return 0;
    }

